Use the following variables
{Name}, {Name2}, {Model}, {Size}, {MPN}, {Description}, {Description2}, {Country}, {Id}, {Barcode}, {ExternalId}, {Currency}, {A1}, {A2}, {A3}, {A4}
Prices: {Price}, {WholesalePrice}, {OldPrice}, {StaticPrice}
Dates: {Date}, {StaticPriceDate}
Use with Discount Labels: {DiscountPrice}, {LowestPrice}, {DiscountNo}, {DiscountStartDate}, {DiscountEndDate}

DATE FORMATS
Any date placeholder takes an optional format after a colon, for example {StaticPriceDate:dd.MM.yyyy}. Without one, dates print in the phone's short format.
dd = day 01-31, d = day 1-31
MM = month 01-12, M = month 1-12, MMM = Jan, MMMM = January
yyyy = 2026, yy = 26
HH = hour 00-23, mm = minutes, ss = seconds
Upper case DD and YYYY work too. Anything else prints as written, so {DiscountEndDate:dd/MM/yyyy} and {DiscountEndDate:dd-MM-yyyy} both work. To print a letter literally, wrap it in single quotes: {StaticPriceDate:'od' dd.MM.yyyy}
A placeholder whose date is empty prints nothing at all.

MATHS
Price placeholders can be used in arithmetic with + - * / and brackets, for example {Price}*0.8 or (1-{DiscountPrice}/{Price})*100
